window.registerTopic('react-eco', {
  why: `The ecosystem questions check breadth: can you add routing, write a test, localise strings, ship a mobile app with the same skills, and choose tooling sensibly? Interviewers rarely go deep here, but they expect correct names and purposes: React Router's components and history, Jest and the renderers, React Intl, React Native, create-react-app, TypeScript or Flow, styled-components, Formik, Next.js.

Most of the repository's Router answers describe **v4**; mention that v6 changed the API (\`Routes\`, \`useNavigate\`, \`useParams\`) to show you keep current.`,
  notes: [
    { h: 'React Router', md: `**React Router** adds screens and flows while keeping the URL in sync with what is displayed. It wraps the **history** library (\`window.history\` for browser and hash histories, plus a memory history for tests and React Native). Router components: \`BrowserRouter\`, \`HashRouter\`, \`MemoryRouter\`; a **\`Switch\`** renders the first matching \`Route\` exclusively (the "Router may have only one child element" warning means you need one wrapper); a \`Route\` without a \`path\` always matches, which is how you build a **NotFound** page. Benefits of v4: everything is a component, no manual history setup, nested routing, \`Link\` and \`NavLink\`.

Navigation: \`history.push(path, state)\` adds an entry, \`history.replace\` overwrites the current one (automatic **redirect after login** uses \`<Redirect>\` or \`replace\`). Programmatic navigation in v4: the \`withRouter\` HOC, the \`history\` prop on route components, or (not recommended) context; in v6, \`useNavigate()\`. Query strings: v4 removed parsing, so use \`URLSearchParams\` or the \`query-string\` package. To access history outside components, create and export a \`history\` object and pass it to a plain \`<Router history={history}>\`. Add Google Analytics by listening to \`history.listen\` for page views.` },
    { h: 'Testing', md: `**Jest** is Facebook's JavaScript testing framework built on Jasmine ideas: automatic test discovery, automatic mocking, async tests written synchronously, parallel sandboxed runs, snapshot testing and a jsdom environment, all with zero configuration.

\`\`\`js
// sum.test.js
const sum = require("./sum");
test("adds 1 + 2 to equal 3", () => { expect(sum(1, 2)).toBe(3); });
\`\`\`

Renderers: **Shallow Renderer** renders one level deep to assert on what \`render\` returns without child behaviour; **TestRenderer** (react-test-renderer) renders to plain JavaScript objects without a DOM, ideal for snapshots; **ReactTestUtils** simulates DOM events against a rendered tree. Modern projects add React Testing Library on top of Jest.` },
    { h: 'Internationalisation: React Intl', md: `**React Intl** (part of FormatJS) provides components and an API for formatting strings, dates, numbers, relative times and plurals in 150+ languages, in browsers and Node. Two ways to format: components (\`<FormattedMessage id="greeting" values={{ name }} />\`, \`<FormattedDate>\`, \`<FormattedNumber>\`) or the imperative API (\`intl.formatMessage\`, \`formatDate\`) obtained via \`injectIntl\`/\`useIntl\`. Components render elements, so for placeholders and \`alt\` text use the API. The current locale is on \`intl.locale\`.` },
    { h: 'React Native', md: `React is a library for web (and server) UIs; **React Native** is a framework that renders real native components on iOS and Android from the same component model (no DOM, \`View\`/\`Text\` instead of div/span, StyleSheet arrays instead of CSS). Test on simulators or on a device via Expo; log with \`console.log\` and \`react-native log-ios\`/\`log-android\`; debug by shaking the device or pressing Cmd+D to open the Chrome debugger at localhost:8081/debugger-ui.` },
    { h: 'Tooling, types and styling', md: `**create-react-app** (react-scripts) bootstraps a project with React, JSX, ES6, TypeScript and Flow support, autoprefixed CSS, a dev server, Jest, production build with hashing, service worker (\`registerServiceWorker\` for offline caching) and no configuration; \`npx create-react-app app --template typescript\` adds TypeScript. **TypeScript** gives static types, interfaces for complex props, IDE support and fewer runtime errors; **Flow** is Facebook's static checker (a superset with annotations stripped at build); **PropTypes** is a runtime, development-only checker and much weaker than either.

Styling: plain CSS with className (recommended starting point), CSS Modules, inline style objects, and **CSS-in-JS** libraries such as **styled-components**, which lets you write real CSS in tagged template literals that become components (\`const Title = styled.h1\\\`color: red;\\\`\`). Add Bootstrap via CDN, as a dependency, or with react-bootstrap/reactstrap; add Font Awesome by installing the package and using its classes; import SVGs as components with react-scripts 2+. **React DevTools** inspects the component tree, props and state (enable "Allow access to file URLs" for local files; the React tab is missing when the page does not use React or the global hook is not set).` },
    { h: 'Frameworks and libraries around React', md: `**Next.js** is a framework for server-rendered and static React apps: server rendering by default, automatic code splitting, file-system routing, styling and prefetching, deployable anywhere Node runs. **Formik** solves form state, validation and submission. **Relay** is Facebook's GraphQL data layer. **Polymer** web components can be used from React by loading the element and treating it like a tag. React vs Angular: library vs full framework, Virtual DOM vs real DOM, one-way vs two-way binding, JSX vs templates, JavaScript vs TypeScript-first. React vs Vue: more flexibility in large apps, easier testing, mobile via React Native, larger community. Sites using React include Facebook, Instagram, WhatsApp, Netflix, Airbnb, Uber, Dropbox and PayPal. Animation packages: React Transition Group, React Motion. Linting: ESLint with eslint-plugin-react and eslint-plugin-react-hooks.` },
  ],
  pitfalls: [
    'Describing React Router v4 APIs (Switch, withRouter, component prop) as current without noting v6 (Routes, element prop, useNavigate, useParams).',
    'Saying Jest is "just like Mocha". Its selling points are zero config, auto-mocking, snapshots and jsdom.',
    'Confusing React Native with "React for mobile web". It renders native widgets, not a WebView.',
    'Calling PropTypes a type system. It is a runtime dev-only checker; TypeScript and Flow are static.',
    'Using FormattedMessage for placeholder or alt text; those need plain strings from the intl API.',
  ],
  model: [
    { q: 'How does React Router keep the UI in sync with the URL?', a: 'It wraps the history library, which listens to the browser\'s address bar and pushState events. A Router component provides the current location through context; Route components compare their path to that location and render their element when it matches (a Switch or Routes picks the first match). Links call history.push instead of reloading, so navigation updates state and the URL together without a round trip.', ref: 'react-129' },
    { q: 'What would you use to test a React component and why Jest?', a: 'Jest as the runner and assertion library because it needs no configuration, auto-discovers tests, mocks modules automatically, runs tests in parallel sandboxes with jsdom, and supports snapshot testing. For rendering I would use React Testing Library (or react-test-renderer for snapshots) to interact with the component the way a user does, and ReactTestUtils or user-event to simulate events.', ref: 'react-149' },
    { q: 'What is the difference between React and React Native?', a: 'React is a JavaScript library for building user interfaces, rendering to the DOM in browsers and to HTML on servers. React Native is a framework that uses the same component model, JSX and state management but renders platform-native components on iOS and Android through a bridge, so the output is a real native app rather than a web page in a WebView. Styling, navigation and some APIs differ, but the mental model and much business logic carry over.', ref: 'react-188_2' },
  ],
  quiz: [
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'Which React Router component renders only the first matching route?', options: ['Route', 'Link', 'Switch (Routes in v6)', 'Redirect'], answer: 2, explain: 'Without it, every matching Route renders.', ref: 'react-135' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'How do you make a NotFound route in React Router v4?', options: ['Set path="404"', 'Use a Route without a path as the last child of Switch', 'Throw an error', 'Use HashRouter'], answer: 1, explain: 'A path-less Route always matches, so it catches everything above it did not.', ref: 'react-137' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'What does `history.replace` do differently from `history.push`?', options: ['Reloads the page', 'Replaces the current history entry instead of adding a new one', 'Opens a new tab', 'Clears the history'], answer: 1, explain: 'Use replace for redirects so Back does not return to the login page.', ref: 'react-132' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'Which library underlies React Router\'s browser and hash histories?', options: ['redux', 'history', 'axios', 'lodash'], answer: 1, explain: 'It also provides memory history for non-browser environments.', ref: 'react-130' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'Which is an advantage of Jest over Jasmine?', options: ['It has no assertions', 'Automatic test discovery, auto-mocking, sandboxed parallel runs and jsdom', 'It only runs in browsers', 'It requires Karma'], answer: 1, explain: 'Plus snapshot testing and zero config.', ref: 'react-150' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'What does the Shallow Renderer do?', options: ['Renders the full DOM', 'Renders one level deep so you can assert on render output without child behaviour', 'Takes screenshots', 'Runs end-to-end tests'], answer: 1, explain: 'TestRenderer renders to plain objects; ReactTestUtils simulates events.', ref: 'react-146' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'How should you format placeholder text with React Intl?', options: ['<FormattedMessage> inside the attribute', 'The imperative API (intl.formatMessage) because components render elements, not strings', 'Hard-code it', 'Use <FormattedNumber>'], answer: 1, explain: 'Attributes need strings.', ref: 'react-143' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'React Native renders…', options: ['HTML inside a WebView', 'Native platform components from React components', 'Canvas graphics', 'Server HTML'], answer: 1, explain: 'View and Text map to native widgets.', ref: 'react-188_2' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'Which statement about Flow and PropTypes is correct?', options: ['Both are runtime checkers', 'Flow is a static type checker; PropTypes checks props at runtime in development', 'PropTypes is stricter', 'Flow only works with classes'], answer: 1, explain: 'TypeScript plays the same role as Flow.', ref: 'react-194' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'What does styled-components let you do?', options: ['Write CSS in tagged template literals that become React components', 'Compile Sass', 'Replace JSX', 'Generate Bootstrap themes'], answer: 0, explain: 'Example: const Title = styled.h1`color: red;`', ref: 'react-202' },
    { type: 'mcq', q: 'Which feature is part of Next.js by default?', options: ['Client-only rendering', 'Server-side rendering, automatic code splitting and file-system routing', 'Redux store', 'GraphQL server'], answer: 1, explain: 'Plus simple deployment and prefetching.', ref: 'react-257' },
    { type: 'short', q: 'Name three ways to navigate programmatically in React Router v4 and the modern equivalent.', a: 'Wrap the component in `withRouter` to receive `history`; use the `history` prop that Route passes to its component; or read it from context (discouraged). In v6 you call `useNavigate()` and invoke `navigate("/path")`.', ref: 'react-133' },
    { type: 'short', q: 'How does create-react-app help, and when would you eject or choose something else?', a: 'It gives a zero-config toolchain: Babel, Webpack, dev server with hot reload, Jest, ESLint, production build, TypeScript support and polyfill options via react-scripts. Eject or move to Vite/Next.js when you need custom bundler configuration, server rendering, or faster builds.', ref: 'react-314' },
    { type: 'short', q: 'Why might the React tab be missing in Chrome DevTools?', a: 'The page is not using React, React failed to connect to the `__REACT_DEVTOOLS_GLOBAL_HOOK__` global (for example when the extension loaded after the page), or for a local `file://` page the extension lacks "Allow access to file URLs".', ref: 'react-201' },
  ],
  cards: [
    { f: 'React Router', b: 'Routing library keeping URL and UI in sync; wraps the history library; BrowserRouter/HashRouter/MemoryRouter.', ref: 'react-129' },
    { f: 'Switch (v4) / Routes (v6)', b: 'Render only the first matching Route; fixes "Router may have only one child" issues.', ref: 'react-135' },
    { f: 'NotFound route', b: 'A Route without a path placed last inside Switch.', ref: 'react-137' },
    { f: 'push vs replace', b: 'push adds a history entry; replace overwrites the current one (use for redirects after login).', ref: 'react-132' },
    { f: 'Programmatic navigation (v4)', b: 'withRouter HOC, history prop, context (discouraged). v6: useNavigate().', ref: 'react-133' },
    { f: 'Query params in v4', b: 'Not parsed by the router; use URLSearchParams or query-string.', ref: 'react-134' },
    { f: 'Jest', b: 'Zero-config test framework: auto discovery, auto mocks, parallel sandboxes, jsdom, snapshots.', ref: 'react-149' },
    { f: 'Shallow vs TestRenderer vs TestUtils', b: 'Shallow: one level deep. TestRenderer: plain objects, snapshots. TestUtils: simulate DOM events.', ref: 'react-146' },
    { f: 'React Intl', b: 'FormatJS library: FormattedMessage/Date/Number components or intl API; plurals; 150+ locales.', ref: 'react-140' },
    { f: 'React vs React Native', b: 'React: library rendering to DOM/HTML. React Native: framework rendering native iOS/Android components.', ref: 'react-188_2' },
    { f: 'create-react-app provides', b: 'Babel, Webpack, dev server, Jest, ESLint, production build, TS/Flow support; react-scripts.', ref: 'react-314' },
    { f: 'TypeScript / Flow / PropTypes', b: 'TS & Flow: static type checkers. PropTypes: runtime dev-only prop validation.', ref: 'react-56' },
    { f: 'styled-components', b: 'CSS-in-JS: tagged template literals produce styled React components.', ref: 'react-202' },
    { f: 'Next.js', b: 'React framework: SSR by default, code splitting, file-system routing, static export.', ref: 'react-257' },
    { f: 'Formik', b: 'Form library: values in/out, validation and errors, submission handling.', ref: 'react-309' },
    { f: 'React DevTools', b: 'Browser extension/standalone app to inspect component tree, props and state.', ref: 'react-196' },
    { f: 'React vs Angular', b: 'Library vs framework; Virtual DOM vs real DOM; one-way vs two-way binding; JSX vs templates.', ref: 'react-200' },
  ],
  coding: [
    { id: 'routes', title: 'Define routes with a NotFound page', prompt: 'Write the JSX for routes `/`, `/about`, `/users/:id` and a catch-all NotFound, in React Router v4 style and then in v6 style.', starter: '// v4: <Switch> ... </Switch>\n// v6: <Routes> ... </Routes>\nconsole.log("write it, then reveal");', solution: '// v4\n<Switch>\n  <Route exact path="/" component={Home} />\n  <Route path="/about" component={About} />\n  <Route path="/users/:id" component={User} />\n  <Route component={NotFound} />\n</Switch>\n\n// v6\n<Routes>\n  <Route path="/" element={<Home />} />\n  <Route path="about" element={<About />} />\n  <Route path="users/:id" element={<User />} />\n  <Route path="*" element={<NotFound />} />\n</Routes>', ref: 'react-137' },
    { id: 'redirect', title: 'Redirect after login', prompt: 'After a successful login, navigate to `/dashboard` so that the Back button does not return to the login page. Show v4 (history) and v6 (useNavigate).', starter: '// v4: props.history.???("/dashboard")\n// v6: const navigate = useNavigate(); navigate(???)\nconsole.log("write it, then reveal");', solution: '// v4\nprops.history.replace("/dashboard");\n// or render <Redirect to="/dashboard" /> when logged in\n\n// v6\nconst navigate = useNavigate();\nnavigate("/dashboard", { replace: true });', ref: 'react-139' },
    { id: 'jest', title: 'Write a Jest test for a pure function and a component', prompt: 'Given `sum(a, b)` and a `<Greeting name />` component, write a Jest unit test and a snapshot test with react-test-renderer.', starter: '// sum.test.js\n// Greeting.test.js\nconsole.log("write it, then reveal");', solution: '// sum.test.js\nconst sum = require("./sum");\ntest("adds 1 + 2 to equal 3", () => {\n  expect(sum(1, 2)).toBe(3);\n});\n\n// Greeting.test.js\nimport renderer from "react-test-renderer";\nimport Greeting from "./Greeting";\ntest("renders the name", () => {\n  const tree = renderer.create(<Greeting name="Evelin" />).toJSON();\n  expect(tree).toMatchSnapshot();\n});', ref: 'react-151' },
  ],
});
